This repository has not been initialised yet.

The presence of this file is what permits the `init` job in
`.github/workflows/lifecycle.yml` to run. That job rewrites the template
author's identity to yours - the licence holder, the funding target, the
documentation footers, and the issue chooser's contact link - and then deletes
this file, which is what stops it ever running again.

ONE THING IT CANNOT DO FOR YOU, and it is not the chooser's link. That link
carries a placeholder precisely because GitHub substitutes nothing in a
contact link and offers no expression syntax there, so initialisation does
the substitution itself. What it cannot do is switch Discussions ON: that is
a repository setting rather than a file, under Settings → General →
Features, and until it is enabled the link 404s. Blank issues are disabled,
which makes that link the chooser's only route for how-to questions, so it
is worth enabling early.

It should fire on its own shortly after generation. GitHub does not reliably
deliver an event when a repository is created from a template, so if nothing
happens, dispatch "🎯 Standards Lifecycle" from the Actions tab. Running it
twice is harmless.

Deleting this file by hand skips initialisation entirely. You would then set
the licence holder in LICENSE, the funding target in .github/FUNDING.yml, and
the documentation footers in README.md and assets/README.md yourself - plus
the placeholder in .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/config.yml, which the search below
does not match. To find every one of the rest:

    git grep -n tannergolden | grep -v tannergolden/standards \
                             | grep -v tannergolden/intelligence

Those two are links to the shared repositories every generated repository
calls, and are correct for everyone - which is why they are filtered out
rather than replaced.
